+# Conversation growth and session continuity: comparative research for Viche
+
+- **Office:** Executive Innovation Office, independent research front.
+- **Date:** 2026-11-19.
+- **Scope:** research only. No production code, no bills, no Compliance investigation, no changes under the canonical `government/`. This note is the only artifact; it is committed on a branch in this worktree.
+- **Question:** mature Free Software approaches to conversation-context growth and session continuity, and the minimal design that fits "durable records, not a conversation, are the memory" (Constitution VIII).
+
+## 1. Baseline, verified from the local artifacts
+
+`government/handover.sh` (POSIX `/bin/sh`, no model call) implements three commands. `due ID` returns 0 only when the appointee is `idle`/`clearexit`, has >0 prompts, and either has accepted `>= ${VICHE_ROTATE_PROMPTS:-150}` prompts or whose last `step_finish` carried `>= ${VICHE_ROTATE_CONTEXT_TOKENS:-120000}` input+cache-read tokens. `write KEY OLD NEW` builds the note from the predecessor's **last three `text` events, each truncated to 900 chars**, plus pointers to `records/`, `records/handoffs/`, the Town Hall cursor, the Antonina board and the state root; it appends a tab-separated line to `rotations.log`. `show KEY ID` injects the note only for the named successor at 0 prompts. The note explicitly says the predecessor's conversation "is NOT available to you and is NOT authority."
+
+Independent recomputation from `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-state/handover/rotations.log` confirms the brief: **95 rotations over 50.5 h**, of which **55 are `executive`** (per-office median gap **30.1 min**, **15 gaps <20 min**); 11 judiciary, 15 `c1a00001`, 2 innovation-centre, the rest single rotations. Actual handover notes are 1.9–3.5 KB (e.g. `executive.md` 2084 B, `c1a00001.md` 2734 B). The successor still re-reads Constitution + `government/LAWS.md` (**110,391 B**) + orders/dialogue/policy from disk each sitting.
+
+**What is already right:** context per model step is bounded; authority is single-sourced in Git; the mechanism has zero model cost; the 24 h whole-box restart (Constitution VIII) makes *some* form of this mandatory regardless of context growth. The open question is whether the **thin note's content** and the **absence of trigger telemetry** are worth changing.
+
+## 2. Five approaches, with Free Software evidence
+
+### 2.1 Checkpoint / restore of agent state
+**Evidence:** LangGraph checkpointers and stores — MIT, <https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence.md>, <https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/checkpointers.md>; OpenHands conversation persistence + pause/resume — MIT, <https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/guides/convo-persistence.md>, <https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/guides/convo-pause-and-resume.md>.
+
+- **Preserves:** serializable graph/conversation state at a step boundary, including in-flight tool calls; resumable from failure (`PostgresSaver`, `SqliteSaver`); thread-scoped state separate from cross-thread `Store` data.
+- **Loses:** nothing in principle, but the snapshot is only as good as the serialisation; OpenHands explicitly excludes dynamically resolved memory text from `base_state.json`.
+- **Cost (documented):** LangGraph warns that "checkpoints accumulate… increase latency and storage costs" and prescribes a pruning/retention cron; `MemorySaver` loses everything on restart. Requires a durable backend (Postgres) and a snapshot/record consistency discipline.
+- **Prerequisites:** serializable state, a store, and a rule for which copy wins when a snapshot and a record disagree. That last rule is the constitutional problem: a snapshot that can be restored becomes a *second* memory competing with records.
+
+### 2.2 Context compaction / hierarchical summarization
+**Evidence:** OpenHands `LLMSummarizingCondenser` — MIT, <https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/guides/context-condenser.md> and architecture <https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/condenser.md>.
+
+- **Preserves:** recent messages verbatim, the first events (system/user goals), and an LLM-generated summary of older events. The conversation object and its identity survive.
+- **Loses:** detail in the summarized region; the summary is lossy and is generated by a model, so it can drift from what actually happened.
+- **Cost:** docs claim "up to 2x reduction in per-turn API costs", stable latency and equivalent-or-better SWE-bench-style task performance — **vendor documentation, not independent measurement**. Requires an extra LLM call at each compaction and a condenser threshold (`max_size`, `keep_first`).
+- **Fit:** poor as an *authority* layer. Viche already has the lossless artifact (Git records); summarizing the conversation would create a cheaper but unauthoritative copy of something the Constitution already says is not authoritative.
+
+### 2.3 Structured / hierarchical memory
+**Evidence:** MemGPT (virtual context management: in-context tier + external tiers, OS-inspired) — paper CC BY 4.0, code released, <https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560>; Letta MemFS — Apache-2.0 (brand-assets exclusion), <https://docs.letta.com/concepts/memfs/index.md>; OpenHands persistent memory — MIT, <https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/guides/persistent-memory.md>; LangGraph `Store` (cross-thread KV) — MIT, cited above.
+
+- **Preserves:** a deliberately small "always hot" index plus deep material fetched on demand. Letta: root `*.md` files load every turn; directories with their own `MEMORY.md` stay out of context; **every edit is git-committed**; **no vector index by default**. OpenHands: `MEMORY.md` indexes capped at **~6,000 characters**, daily logs read on demand, re-read from disk each session, **excluded from conversation persistence**, and deliberately wrapped in `<UNTRUSTED_CONTENT>` — memory is a *hint*, not truth.
+- **Loses:** whatever the index omitted, and any detail the agent never promoted into it. Requires the agent to maintain the index (prune stale lines, merge duplicates) — a recurring behavioural cost.
+- **Cost:** no service needed if using Markdown + git + file tools; vector/semantic search is an optional add-on. The 6 KB cap and the "untrusted" framing are the two transferable design facts.
+- **Fit:** strongest conceptual match. This is Viche's model stated explicitly: small hot note, cold authoritative records, on-demand reading. Viche's note is currently ~2 KB (under OpenHands' cap) but its *content* is opportunistic rather than index-like.
+
+### 2.4 Event sourcing: append-only log + materialized view / snapshot
+**Evidence:** Temporal event history, replay and Continue-As-New — MIT, <https://docs.temporal.io/workflows.md>, <https://docs.temporal.io/encyclopedia/event-history.md>, <https://docs.temporal.io/workflow-execution/continue-as-new.md>, <https://docs.temporal.io/workflow-execution/limits.md>. Git itself is the same pattern: append-only content-addressed log plus a materialized working tree.
+
+- **Preserves:** a complete ordered log as the **source of truth**. Temporal reconstructs state by *replaying* the history, not by restoring a memory snapshot; side effects (LLM calls, I/O) run once inside Activities and their results are reused on replay. `Continue-As-New` passes latest state into a fresh execution with a fresh history — structurally the same act as Viche's rotation.
+- **Loses:** convenience; replay demands deterministic code and recorded time/randomness, and the history is bounded — **51,200 events / 50 MB, warning at 10,240 / 10 MB**.
+- **Cost:** a durable service and deterministic workflow code; LLM agents violate determinism by default, so each external call must be isolated as a recorded event.
+- **Fit:** Viche already has the cheap version: Git commits (`records/`) are the append-only log, the checkout is the materialized view, and `rotations.log` is a tiny event log of continuity itself. Importing Temporal would add a service and a determinism constraint to gain guarantees Viche does not need — records are already authoritative by Constitution, not by replay.
+
+### 2.5 Viche's current "fresh session + thin handover note"
+- **Preserves:** bounded per-step context, single authority (Git), 24 h-restart compatibility, zero model calls, no new dependencies.
+- **Loses:** in-flight intent not yet written to records; the *reason* behind a decision; which items are open versus merely recently discussed. The note is three truncated free-text reports — a sample of the predecessor's last words, not an index of state.
+- **Cost:** no engineering cost; measured **95 rotations / 50.5 h**, with 15 short (<20 min) executive gaps that may be churn, but the log records **no trigger reason**, so this cannot be diagnosed. Each successor re-reads 110 KB of law.
+- **Prerequisites:** the predecessor actually wrote durable records; the note is a pointer and is non-authoritative (both are stated in the note).
+
+### 2.6 The convergence
+Every mature system above lands on the same three layers: **(a)** an append-only, authoritative record; **(b)** a small, bounded, *non-authoritative* index injected at session start (OpenHands ~6 KB `MEMORY.md`, Letta root memory, Viche ~2 KB note); **(c)** on-demand reads of deep material, with a snapshot/restart at a natural boundary (Temporal Continue-As-New, Viche rotation). Viche's architecture is therefore not defective; the defect is that layer (b) carries prose rather than state, and there is no telemetry to tune layer (c).
+
+## 3. Minimal recommendation
+
+**Keep rotation. Change the note's content generator and log one extra fact. Add no subsystem.**
+
+1. In `handover.sh write`, derive the note deterministically from artifacts that already exist, instead of sampling the last three text events. Keep it ≤4 KB and keep it explicitly non-authoritative. Suggested sections:
+   - `OPEN` — unfinished items from `records/handoffs/` (status fields already exist: `.txt` / `.done` / `.superseded`) and any office obligation file;
+   - `DONE / DO-NOT-REPEAT` — recently closed items plus the Town Hall cursor and board issues already posted to (the exact facts that prevent duplicate external actions);
+   - `RE-READ` — pointers to the specific record paths (and commit SHAs where cheap) the successor must verify before acting;
+   - `RECENT NARRATIVE` — at most the last 1–2 reports, demoted to context, never to authority.
+2. In `rotations.log`, append three fields written from values already computed by `due` and available at `write`: **trigger** (`prompts` / `context` / `both`), **prompt count**, **last context tokens**. This is the missing measurement; it costs a few bytes per line.
+3. Add one sentence to the note asking the successor's first report to acknowledge the note and list its `OPEN` items. No enforcement mechanism, no new receipt queue — just an instruction consistent with existing reporting policy.
+
+**Do not add:** an LLM summarizer (extra model call, lossy authority), a checkpoint backend (second memory + pruning), a vector store (no evidence Viche's grep-able records need one), or Temporal (service + determinism constraint). Each is more expensive than the baseline and none is demonstrably cheaper.
+
+### Cost comparison
+
+| Option | Engineering / recurring cost | What it actually fixes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| No-change baseline | 0; 95 rotations/50.5 h, 15 short executive gaps, trigger unknown | Nothing new; already bounds context |
+| Tune the two thresholds only | ~2 env vars; no code | Changes *frequency* only. Raising 150/120000 lowers churn but pushes per-step context toward the cap and increases 110 KB re-reads; lowering increases churn. With no trigger telemetry this is blind tuning. |
+| Recommended | ~40–60 lines of POSIX `/bin/sh` in one existing script + 3 log columns; deterministic, no model calls, no services, ~+100 B/rotation | Replaces opportunistic prose with state; makes duplicate-work and churn measurable; enables *evidence-based* threshold tuning later |
+
+## 4. Falsifiers — observations that would show this is NOT an improvement
+
+1. Over ≥100 further rotations, the rate of repeated external actions or re-opened already-closed work does not fall (or rises) relative to the baseline.
+2. With trigger telemetry enabled, <10% of rotations are context-driven and the rest are prompt-driven with healthy gaps — i.e. context growth was never the binding constraint, so the premise fails and only threshold policy deserves work.
+3. The structured digest measurably adds latency or tokens per rotation while no failure mode improves: it is net cost.
+4. Successors' first reports never acknowledge the `OPEN` list — structure that is not read cannot help.
+5. The `DO-NOT-REPEAT` section causes successors to skip legitimate re-verification from records; that would violate the duty to verify and make the change harmful.
+
+## 5. Limits
+
+- **Same-model limitation.** All evidence comes from LLM-agent harnesses of the same generation; there is no cross-model evidence that continuity benefits transfer. Antonina selects and changes Viche's models, so no model-specific assumption is made here.
+- **Small evidence base.** 95 rotations in 50.5 h from one town, no control group, no randomized comparison; `rotations.log` omits the trigger, so the reported gaps and medians cannot be attributed. Note sizes are point-in-time snapshots.
+- **Documentation vs measurement only.** OpenHands' "2x" is a vendor doc claim; LangGraph's growth/pruning warning and Temporal's 51,200/50 MB limits are documented or enforced properties, not agent-quality outcomes; MemGPT's evaluation covers document analysis and multi-session chat, not governance duties. No benefits are claimed as measured for Viche.
+- This was research only: no production file was changed, no bill-sponsored or drafted, and no Judiciary compliance investigation was performed.
